<Project>

  <!--
    Ships FrameLedger.Overlay.dll beside the importing project's output.

    §S22. The guard now refuses a payload that does not resolve into the
    directory the guard's own code was loaded from — so the Overlay must LAND
    there, or the constraint is a gate that cannot pass and the product refuses
    its own injection.

    Nothing copied the Overlay anywhere before this file existed. It was built
    into build/native/x64-release/FrameLedger.Overlay/ and left there, so
    `dotnet publish` produced an out/app containing the Agent, the guard and the
    rules seed but no payload at all. That was invisible while the Agent had no
    injector control, and it would have surfaced as an unexplained
    kPayloadNotOurs the first time one was wired — which is the diagnosis §S22
    warns is easy to misread as the guard being over-eager.

    Imported by FrameLedger.Agent only, and for the same reason
    FrameLedger.Guard.targets is: the Agent is the sole host of the guard (§S18
    blocker 3, ratified). FrameLedger.App has no injector and must not carry a
    payload it cannot use.

    NOT imported by the native test suite. src/native/tests/CMakeLists.txt stages
    its own copy beside fl_guard_test, because there the guard is compiled INTO
    the test binary and "the guard's own directory" is the test's directory.

    UNEXERCISED TODAY, stated rather than implied: the Agent builds no host, no
    watcher and no injector control (Program.cs seeds the rules file and exits),
    so nothing in a published build injects anything yet. This places the payload
    where the gate will look when that lands; it does not make injection work.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <FlNativeOverlayDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\native\x64-release\FrameLedger.Overlay\</FlNativeOverlayDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(FlNativeOverlayDir)FrameLedger.Overlay.dll"
          Condition="Exists('$(FlNativeOverlayDir)FrameLedger.Overlay.dll')">
      <Link>FrameLedger.Overlay.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Warn rather than fail, exactly as FrameLedger.Guard.targets does: build.ps1
    runs the native build first, so by the time this evaluates the DLL exists. A
    hard error would make `dotnet build` on a fresh clone fail for a reason that
    has nothing to do with the managed code.
  -->
  <Target Name="FlWarnOnMissingOverlay" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="!Exists('$(FlNativeOverlayDir)FrameLedger.Overlay.dll')">
    <Warning Text="FrameLedger.Overlay.dll was not found at $(FlNativeOverlayDir). Run build.ps1 native first; the guard refuses a payload that is not beside it (§S22)." />
  </Target>

</Project>
